<p class="lt-chem-36403">Addition of Grignard reagents converts esters to two alcohols, one 3<sup>o</sup> alcohols (main product) and one 1<sup>o</sup> alcohol (considered a side product). The Grignard reagent adds to the ester twice, once during a nucleophilic acyl substitution to form a ketone intermediate then again during a nucleophilic addition to form the 3<sup>o</sup> alcohol product. Overall, during this reaction two C-C bonds are formed on the ester&rsquo;s original carbonyl carbon.   </p>

<div mt-section-origin="Bookshelves/Organic_Chemistry/Organic_Chemistry_(Morsch_et_al.)/21:_Carboxylic_Acid_Derivatives-_Nucleophilic_Acyl_Substitution_Reactions/21.06:_Chemistry_of_Esters" class="mt-section" id="section_27"><span id="General_Reaction_6"></span><h4 class="lt-chem-36403 editable">General Reaction</h4>



