</div><div mt-section-origin="Bookshelves/Organic_Chemistry/Organic_Chemistry_(Morsch_et_al.)/21:_Carboxylic_Acid_Derivatives-_Nucleophilic_Acyl_Substitution_Reactions/21.06:_Chemistry_of_Esters" class="mt-section" id="section_30"><span id="Mechanism_4"></span><h4 class="lt-chem-36403 editable">Mechanism</h4>

<p class="lt-chem-36403">In the first two steps of the mechanism, the OR leaving group from the ester is replaced by the R group from the Grignard reagent through a nucleophilic acyl substitution. This forms a ketone intermediate which is not isolated because ketones, which are more reactive than esters, rapidly undergo nucleophilic addition with a second equivalent of the Grignard reagent to form an alkoxide intermediate. An acid work-up protonates the alkoxide to form the 3<sup>o</sup> alcohol product.</p>

